 ## Background
-The concept of splatting was introduced by Westin et al. in 1992 as a volume rendering technique that projects 3D data onto the 2D image plane using kernel functions [S1][S3, unofficial][S4]. The specific formulation for 3D Gaussian Splatting and its associated sorting/compositing rules, particularly within the context of the glTF extension, was formalized by the Khronos Group [S1]. The sorting mechanism typically relies on radix sort for performance, ordering splats back-to-front based on the distance between the camera position and the splat's mean vector in global space [S1]. The compositing equation $C = \sum_{i \in \mathscr{N}} c_i \alpha_i \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \alpha_j)$ defines the final pixel color, where $\mathscr{N}$ is the set of contributing splats, $c_i$ is the color, and $\alpha_i$ is the effective opacity derived from the splat's opacity attribute and the projected 2D Gaussian's probability density function [S1].
+The concept of splatting as an object-order volume rendering technique was introduced in the early 1990s, with foundational work by Westover (1990) and Levoy (1990) establishing the use of kernel functions to project volumetric data onto the image plane [S6, unofficial][S10, unofficial]. The specific application of sorting and alpha compositing for Gaussian primitives has become central to modern 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) techniques, popularized by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1]. The Khronos Group's `KHR_gaussian_splatting` extension formalized the sorting and composition rules for interoperable rendering of Gaussian splats, specifying back-to-front sorting based on camera-to-mean distance and the standard alpha blending equation [S1]. Recent developments have focused on optimizing this step through hierarchical sorting (Stop-the-pop) [S1][S4], order-independent transparency [S1], and training-time stability mechanisms (Detached Hybrid Transparency) [S1].
 
